 ## Background
-The concept of explicit radiance fields originated with the need to accelerate NeRF training and rendering. Early methods like [Neural Volumes](912-neural-volumes.md) used dense voxel grids but suffered from high memory costs. The introduction of [Plenoxels](7440-plenoxels.md) demonstrated that replacing MLPs with sparse voxel grids storing spherical harmonics could achieve 100x faster training [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. Subsequent works like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization](4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md) (DVGO) and [Ref-DVGO](6572-ref-dvgo.md) refined this by introducing sparse voxel structures and analytical gradients. The [Coarse-to-Fine](1153-coarse-to-fine-optimization-with-pruning.md) and [PlenOctree](1291-plenoctree-optimization.md) approaches further optimized memory and quality by dynamically adapting resolution. [SNeRG](806-explicit-radiance-fields.md) introduced block-based hierarchical structures to mitigate octree traversal overhead [S1, unofficial]. [CBARF](2568-sparse-voxel-grid-optimization.md) applied density voxel grids to robust pose optimization [S2]. [Multi-scale Neural Field Reconstruction](7437-multi-scale-neural-field-reconstruction.md) extended the concept to pyramid-structured encoding volumes for dynamic scenes [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of explicit voxel grids for radiance fields emerged as a solution to the slow training and inference speeds of implicit Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF). Plenoxels [S1, S2, S4, S5] was a pivotal work that demonstrated that replacing MLPs with sparse voxel grids storing density and spherical harmonic coefficients could achieve training speeds two orders of magnitude faster than NeRF. Subsequent works like DVGO [S2] and Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization [S1, unofficial] further refined this by introducing sparsity, Morton code sorting, and dynamic pruning to handle unbounded scenes and reduce memory usage. Hierarchical strategies, such as Coarse-to-Fine Optimization [S1, unofficial] and PlenOctree Optimization [S1, unofficial], were developed to manage the trade-off between resolution and computational cost by starting with coarse representations and refining them. Explicit Radiance Fields (PERF) [S1, unofficial] took a different approach by using analytical Gauss-Newton solvers instead of gradient descent, assuming diffuse surfaces to accelerate convergence.
 
